On Wed, Oct 10, 2007 at 11:59:06AM +0200, A. Pagaltzis wrote:
} * Juerd Waalboer  [2007-10-08 13:11]:
} > sub refaddr($) {
} >   my $pkg = ref($_[0]) or return undef;
} >   if (blessed($_[0])) {
} >     bless $_[0], 'Scalar::Util::Fake';
} >   }
} >   else {
} >     $pkg = undef;
} >   }
} >   "$_[0]" =~ /0x(\\w+)/;
} >   my $i = do { local $^W; hex $1 };
} >   bless $_[0], $pkg if defined $pkg;
} >   $i;
} > }
} 
} FWIW, having established that there’s no better way to write that
} function, I really dislike the way that spreads the reblessing
} logic around the function based on a flag. Is there any reason
} not to write it this way?
} 
}     sub refaddr($) {
}       my $pkg = ref($_[0]) or return undef;
} 
}       my $addr;
}       if (blessed($_[0])) {

You're treating the return result of ref() and blessed() as a boolean
and this is not true for all perl classes. This function is incorrect
in the face of the '0' and "\\0" classes.

defined( blessed( ... ) ) is a boolean test that works.

  sub refaddr (...) {
      my $pkg = blessed( $_[0] );

      my $addr;
      if ( defined $pkg ) {
          bless, $_[0], 'Scalar::Util::Fake'
          $addr = 0+$_[0];
          bless, $_[0], $pkg;
      }
      elsif ( ref $_[0] ) {
          $addr = 0+$_[0];
      }
      return $addr;
  }

Also, I simultaneously apologise for ever advocating for the existence
of those two classes. Blech. I shouldn't have.

Josh

On Mon, 2007-11-12 at 17:35 +0100, Rafael Garcia-Suarez wrote:
> Here's a list of dual-lived modules in the core that have patches that
> aren't yet integrated in the CPAN version. It would be nice to release
> versions on CPAN with those changes in, in order to distribute perl
> 5.10 with "authorized" versions of core modules.
> 
> We've already delayed too much the release of 5.10, so, with the
> exception of the latest syncs to Jos' modules, (a security fix to
> Archive::Tar and a warning fix in CPANPLUS::Dist::Build), I'd like to
> stop accepting code patches now, except the ones that bump $VERSION
> variables to non-alpha version numbers. VMS portability will be
> achieved later. The general opinion is that File::Spec really needs
> some rewrite (or replacement) here.
> 
> Note that it's not important that you actually release a version of
> your dual life module to CPAN, just make sure that if you release one
> with the same version number as what comes with the core, both
> versions match. In other words, it's sufficient to "burn" a version
> number for the 5.10 core to use.

I think the patch taking Term::Cap to the version on CPAN got missed a
couple of weeks ago:

--- /home/jonathan/devel/perl/perl-current/lib/Term/Cap.pm      2007-10-19 11:24:22.000000000 +0100
+++ /home/jonathan/devel/modules/Term-Cap/Cap.pm        2007-10-25 09:34:41.000000000 +0100
@@ -19,7 +19,7 @@
 use vars qw($VERSION $VMS_TERMCAP);
 use vars qw($termpat $state $first $entry);

-$VERSION = '1.10';
+$VERSION = '1.11';

 # Version undef: Thu Dec 14 20:02:42 CST 1995 by hqnpedl@bsdi.com
 # Version 1.00:  Thu Nov 30 23:34:29 EST 2000 by ftnhszc@pobox.com
@@ -53,6 +53,8 @@
 #       Don't use try to use $ENV{HOME} if it doesn't exist
 #       Give Win32 'dumb' if TERM isn't set
 #       Provide fallback 'dumb' termcap entry as last resort
+# Version 1.11: Thu Oct 25 09:33:07 BST 2007
+#       EBDIC fixes from Chun Bing Ge 

 # TODO:
 # support Berkeley DB termcaps
@@ -418,18 +420,36 @@
             # print STDERR "DEBUG: string $1 = $2\\n";
             next if defined $self->{ '_' . ( $cap = $1 ) };
             $_ = $2;
-            s/\\\\E/\\033/g;
-            s/\\\\(\\d\\d\\d)/pack('c',oct($1) & 0177)/eg;
-            s/\\\\n/\\n/g;
-            s/\\\\r/\\r/g;
-            s/\\\\t/\\t/g;
-            s/\\\\b/\\b/g;
-            s/\\\\f/\\f/g;
-            s/\\\\\\^/\\377/g;
-            s/\\^\\?/\\177/g;
-            s/\\^(.)/pack('c',ord($1) & 31)/eg;
-            s/\\\\(.)/$1/g;
-            s/\\377/^/g;
+            if ( ord('A') == 193 )
+            {
+               s/\\\\E/\\047/g;
+               s/\\\\(\\d\\d\\d)/pack('c',oct($1) & 0177)/eg;
+               s/\\\\n/\\n/g;
+               s/\\\\r/\\r/g;
+               s/\\\\t/\\t/g;
+               s/\\\\b/\\b/g;
+               s/\\\\f/\\f/g;
+               s/\\\\\\^/\\337/g;
+               s/\\^\\?/\\007/g;
+               s/\\^(.)/pack('c',ord($1) & 31)/eg;
+               s/\\\\(.)/$1/g;
+               s/\\337/^/g;
+            }
+            else
+            {
+               s/\\\\E/\\033/g;
+               s/\\\\(\\d\\d\\d)/pack('c',oct($1) & 0177)/eg;
+               s/\\\\n/\\n/g;
+               s/\\\\r/\\r/g;
+               s/\\\\t/\\t/g;
+               s/\\\\b/\\b/g;
+               s/\\\\f/\\f/g;
+               s/\\\\\\^/\\377/g;
+               s/\\^\\?/\\177/g;
+               s/\\^(.)/pack('c',ord($1) & 31)/eg;
+               s/\\\\(.)/$1/g;
